  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to the reimbursement of school districts for the transportation of students",
    "basePrintNo": "S7878",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7878-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 17,
       "fullName": "Martin Malavé Dilan",
       "imgName": "426_martin_malave_dilan.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 426,
       "sessionMemberId": 56,
       "sessionYear": 2009,
       "shortName": "DILAN"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §3635, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7878\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to the reimbursement of\nschool districts for the transportation of students\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis legislation would require a city with a population of one million\nor more to provide reimbursement for the cost of providing\ntransportation after 5:00 PM if the nonpublic or public school offers\nregularly scheduled academic classes until 5:00 PM or later.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 would amend section 3635 of the education law by adding a\nnew subdivision 8 that would require a city school district located in\na city with a population of one million or more to reimburse the cost\nof providing transportation by a means other than public\ntransportation after S:oo PM ifthe nonpublic or public school offers\nregularly scheduled academic classes until 5:00 PM or later. To be\neligible for reimbursement, a school must begin classes no later than\n9:30 AM.\n\nEligible students would be those in grades K-2 who live at least\none-half mile from their school and those in grades 3-6 who live at\nleast one mile from their school. In order for a student's\ntransportation to be eligible for reimbursement, the parent or\nguardian would be required to submit a request by April 1.\n\nThe cost to the school district of reimbursing the school or parents\nor guardians shall be eligible for state aid in the same manner and at\nthe same rate as other transportation services.\n\nThe school district would not be required to pay more in\nreimbursements than it would spend, including capital costs, to\nprovide transportation for such students if it was providing such\ntransportation directly.\n\nThe Chancellor of New York City Schools would be required to\npromulgate regulations to implement this provision.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nIn New York City, some schools have academic course schedules that run\nfrom 9:30 AM or earlier to 5:00 PM or later. Students walking or\nriding an MTA bus or subway home after 5:00 PM face dangers due to\nrush hour traffic. Moreover, the sun has already set by 5:00 PM for\nmuch of the school year, adding dim conditions to the hazards facing\nthese students. Therefore, parents have to choose between allowing\nyoung children to travel alone at night in heavy traffic or expending\nwork time and tight money to provide daily escorts so that their\nchildren can have the best education possible. Particularly in these\ntroubled economic times, parents face many difficult choices as they\nstruggle to make ends meet without limiting their children's\nopportunities.\n\nThis bill would compensate them for those costs and ensure that\nchildren are not put at risk on their trip home. The authority to\ndetermine how best to implement this program is placed in the hands of\nthe Chancellor of the New York City School District, as many of the\nlogistical questions are best addressed by those with local knowledge.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nThis is a new bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nThe cost is capped at the current cost to the city to provide\ntransportation for these students, so there should not be additional\ncost.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on the first of July, 2010.\n",
